WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.940
- Thanks to Mack Weldon for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.573
and helping me look fly.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.900
President Trump caused quite a stir

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.820
when he tweeted that the
election should be delayed

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.820
because Democrats were about
to commit mass voter fraud

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.090
by encouraging people to vote by mail.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:17.060
Now I've already covered the
possibility of election delays

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.810
in my video, "Can the
President Cancel the Election?"

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.240
And I and most people
came to the conclusion

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.630
that because the election is
enshrined in the constitution,

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:26.470
that President Trump

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.410
probably cannot delay
or cancel the election.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.760
But President Trump's recent
comments about voting by mail,

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.940
raise a different issue entirely

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:37.630
not canceling the election
or postponing the election,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.460
but voter suppression.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.010
And according to the President,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.970
voting by absentee ballot is safe

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.830
while voting by mail is unsafe.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.480
Apparently unaware that those
are basically the same thing.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.390
Then the Trump Campaign recently
sued the state of Nevada

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:56.770
for its plans to send every
registered voter a ballot.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.740
Now, why are states suddenly interested

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.320
in increasing opportunities
to vote by mail?

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.340
And are there legal insecurity differences

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.370
in how mailed ballots and absentee ballots

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.020
are handled and counted?

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.040
And does the president have the power

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.930
to change the way that
states run their elections?

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.860
Well, today we're going
to learn about the laws

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.200
that regulate elections,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.560
the prevalence of voter
fraud or lack thereof,

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.624
and the safety of mail-in
votes and absentee ballots.

00:01:22.624 --> 00:01:25.650
(upbeat music)

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.700
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.990
because we have an election coming up

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.700
and the presence of the COVID-19 pandemic

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.930
has changed absolutely everything.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.380
And I never thought I'd say this,

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.130
but it appears that the
president is trying to get people

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.880
not to vote in the November election.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.870
- I've said it before,
and I'll say it again.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.090
Democracy simply doesn't work.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.560
- So the first question we need to ask

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.020
is who regulates voting in general?

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.300
Well, there's actually a fair amount

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.780
of federalism in voting.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.640
The U.S. Constitution Elections Clause,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.160
Article one, Section four,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.187
empowers the states to
determine the quote,

00:02:00.187 --> 00:02:01.730
"Time, place and manner"

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.620
of congressional elections
subject to Congress's authority

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.130
to make or alter state regulations.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.620
Dividing the power between the
states and federal government

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.960
is a cornerstone of American federalism.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.060
state and local governments
therefore have the authority

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.350
to enact laws for such elections,

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.630
including rules concerning public notices,

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.680
voter registration, voter protection,

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.640
fraud protection, vote counting

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.930
and determination of election results.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.290
However, Congress may pass federal laws,

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.320
regulating congressional elections,

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.960
which then automatically preempt

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.820
any contrary state statutes.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.020
And Congress can also
enact its own regulations,

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.610
if the states didn't
address something important,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.810
but remember both Congress
and state governments

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.560
are constrained by the other protections

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.690
guaranteed by the constitution.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.280
For example, if a state
imposes barriers to voting

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.100
that violate the 14th Amendment,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.910
the laws can be challenged
as unconstitutional.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.270
During the Jim Crow era,

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.670
states enacted laws designed
to stop black voters

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.950
from voting such as literacy tests,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.360
property ownership requirements,

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.410
and mandatory of fees known as poll taxes.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:02.243
But for the most part,

00:03:02.243 --> 00:03:04.270
the laws that regulate how a person votes

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.740
such as what time the
polls open and close,

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:08.210
the deadline for registering

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:09.970
or the assignment of polling places

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:11.420
are all examples of decisions

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.550
that the Elections Clause
of the constitution

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.560
allows for the states to make.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.590
So the vast majority of
laws that govern elections

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.620
are handled by the states
and neither the president,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.860
the executive or Congress
has anything to really say

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.600
about that particular regulation.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.890
But what about election security?

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.170
Congress has never passed
significantly legislation

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.530
mandating security
procedures for paper ballots

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.460
or electronic ballots,
even elections equipment,

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.370
including the software
from private vendors

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:39.640
is mostly unregulated.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.490
And in the absence of federal laws,

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.180
states have developed their
own security protocols.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.090
Many experts have argued that Congress

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.590
should probably step in and
regulate security procedures

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.940
to create uniformity amongst the states,

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.660
including federal agencies,

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:56.170
and actually in November of
2019, several federal agencies,

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:57.450
including Homeland and Security,

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.350
the Justice Department and the FBI

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:00.470
released a joint statement,

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.127
naming election security as quote,

00:04:02.127 --> 00:04:04.640
"A top priority for the
United states government."

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.580
These agencies were chiefly concerned

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.670
about threats from foreign
countries and other bad actors.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.140
Congress did not pass an
election security bill

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:12.973
after this warning.

00:04:12.973 --> 00:04:14.610
And when Democrats tried to add funding

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.540
for voter security measures to legislation

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.450
addressing the COVID-19 crisis,

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.000
specifically targeting the
safety of voting by mail,

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.870
President Trump described it as a scam

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.970
to stop Republicans
from winning objection,

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.007
stating quote,

00:04:26.007 --> 00:04:27.910
"You'd never have a Republican elected

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.680
in this country again if
the money for mail-in voting

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.620
was included in the bill."

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.760
And there is some truth to
the President's statement,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.080
even if it is a crass political truth,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.050
which is that research tends to show

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.110
that when voter turnout goes up,

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.570
it favors the Democrats and
disfavors the Republicans,

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:47.630
but saying that you actually
want lower voter turnout

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:50.310
is a bit like saying
the quiet part out loud,

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.390
but that takes us to the COVID-19 crisis.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.570
Now, before the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:57.910
the main concern about the
security of voting systems

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.940
was the software and
hardware used to tally, scan,

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:05.610
collate and report votes could
be manipulated or hacked.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.980
And truthfully that probably
deserves its own video

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.250
because our electronic voting systems

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:12.950
are woefully inadequate

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.780
and really need additional
security measures.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.140
But the primary concern changed

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.380
when the COVID-19 pandemic hit.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.760
No longer were people so worried

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.820
about the electronic voting at
actual formal polling places,

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:28.760
but instead, people didn't
want to leave their homes.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.510
And since we don't have a system

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.960
of being able to vote
electronically from home,

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.740
the emphasis changed to voting
by mail and absentee ballots.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:38.850
And in a way, this seemed
like a perfect solution.

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.630
People could vote from home,

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:42.990
minimum using the risk of transmitting

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.330
or receiving the COVID-19 virus

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:48.560
and many politicians on
both sides of the aisle

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.330
and many different states were
trying to encourage people

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:52.490
to vote by mail.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.000
Now I have been using the terms,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.440
absentee voting and voting by
mail almost interchangeably,

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.860
but let's talk about the
difference between those two terms.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:02.500
Practically speaking, as a
regard to an individual ballot,

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:03.680
there's almost no difference,

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:05.580
if you vote by mail or vote by absentee,

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.280
you are taking your ballot,

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.380
you're filling it out at
home or some other place,

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.861
and you're mailing it into the place

00:06:11.861 --> 00:06:12.800
it's going to register your vote.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.330
Really, the only difference
is in terms of scale,

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.320
an absentee ballot is sort of an exception

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:18.153
to the general rule.

00:06:18.153 --> 00:06:20.960
If you're unable to go to
a physical polling place

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.280
or you live outside of
the state temporarily,

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.500
then you can ask for an absentee ballot,

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.140
you can fill it out and then
send it into the polling place

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:29.410
as an exception to the general rule

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:31.010
that you would have to go in in person.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:32.910
Voting by mail is
effectively the same thing,

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.220
just a difference of scope.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:37.860
Instead of asking for an
absentee ballot as an exception,

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:39.770
everyone can vote by mail

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.900
regardless of whether
they meet the criteria

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.460
for an absentee ballot or not.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.130
And states differ in
terms of whether you need

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:50.470
to formally register ahead
of time for a mail-in ballot,

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:53.270
or whether the state will just
send every registered voter,

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:56.400
a mail-in ballot without
having to preregister.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:57.770
And while voting by mail

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:59.290
is certainly getting
a lot of scrutiny now,

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:00.460
it's definitely nothing new.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:03.260
Some states, including
Washington, Utah and Colorado

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:06.270
have been voting only by
mail for several years.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.020
Initially mail-in voting was supported

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:09.860
by both Republicans and Democrats.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:13.010
However, President Trump was
disturbed by this development

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:14.340
and he started tweeting his concerns

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.340
about alleged differences

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:18.290
between absentee ballots and vote by mail.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:19.240
In the President's mind,

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.480
it appears that absentee ballots are good

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.980
and mail-in ballots are bad.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.950
- And you get thousands
and thousands of people

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:28.510
sitting in somebody's living room,

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.600
signing ballots all over the place.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:34.180
No, I think that mail-in
voting is a terrible thing.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:35.890
- Does that really happen?

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:37.280
The short answer is, no,

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.870
because there's little
practical difference

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.060
between the absentee
ballots and mailed ballots.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.910
President Trump's own lawyers
even admit this in a lawsuit,

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:46.170
challenging Pennsylvania's
mail-in voting rules.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:47.567
The President's campaign says, the quote,

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:49.280
"The terms 'mail-in' and 'absentee'

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.600
are used interchangeably
to discuss the use

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.190
of the United states Postal Service

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:55.760
to deliver ballots to and from elections."

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.250
So let's dig in a little bit deeper

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:59.300
into the various states voting laws.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.440
All states allow voters who have a reason

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:02.810
they can't vote on election day

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:04.740
to request a ballot in advance.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.930
Well, a majority of states
currently let all voters

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.480
ask for a ballot in advance
without needing an excuse

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.490
for why the person can't vote in person.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.770
And some states adhere to
the traditional concept

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:14.760
of an absentee ballot.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.980
An absentee ballot
technically is an old term

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.560
that meant someone was voting by mail

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.270
because on election day
they would be absent

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.100
from the jurisdiction in which they lived.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:25.070
And 16 states still only
permit certain voters

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.250
to request an absentee ballot by mail

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:29.770
when they have an excuse
for not being able to vote

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:31.130
at the polls on election day.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:33.710
Either that they won't be in
the jurisdiction on that day

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:36.080
or that they have an
illness or disability.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.670
This is the traditional
meeting of voting absentee.

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:39.660
In all states,

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:42.380
military voters who are
mostly covered by federal law

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.640
are permitted to vote absentee.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.250
The states have a range of other excuses

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:48.670
that justify voting absentee
like medical emergency,

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:51.730
confinement to jail or
confinement to medical facility,

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.470
and many states also give special help

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:55.210
to those who can't vote in person.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:56.710
For example, in Arizona,

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:58.830
election officials will
deliver an absentee ballot

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.920
to voters confined due
to a medical emergency,

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.490
but that's in contrast to what's called

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.630
No-Excuse mailed ballots.

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:07.570
Delays in problem at polling locations

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:09.310
frequently cause the kind
of disturbing problems

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:10.660
with that we see on election day,

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:11.780
long lines,

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.240
broken electronic voting machines,

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.470
and people turned away
because time ran out.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:17.500
state legislatures address
some of these problems

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.470
by expanding the number of
days for early in-person voting

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:22.520
and eliminating the
restrictions as to who can vote

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:24.300
absentee or by mail.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.630
This is why 34 states in Washington DC

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:30.150
have what's called No-Excuse
absentee or mail-in voting.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:31.890
And a quick note on terminology

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:33.570
since state laws are not identical,

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.880
you may see states using
different terminology

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.260
like advanced ballots,

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:38.320
mailed ballots,

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:39.760
by mail ballots,

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.110
and vote by mail ballots.

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:42.430
Although different terms are used

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:44.090
generally, they mean the same thing.

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:47.340
And although the word mail is
used in almost all of them,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.140
this means that the ballots
are mailed to the voter.

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:52.160
Voters can send them back via the mail,

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.010
but all states also allow
the voters to drop them off

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:56.800
at designated secure locations.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:59.580
Voters who live in these
states don't need to claim

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.420
that they will be out of the jurisdiction

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:03.520
or that they have any kind
of illness or emergency

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.520
that makes it impossible
for them to vote in person.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.700
And some states like Oregon, Washington,

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:10.560
Hawaii and Colorado
automatically send a ballot

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.010
to every registered voter
and some people hate this.

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:17.180
But now is probably a good time to talk

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:20.110
about some of the misconceptions
about the security

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:23.710
or security problems of
No-Excuse mail-in ballots.

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:26.960
The main claim that President
Trump and various Republicans

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.630
are making is that
No-Excuse absentee ballots

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:31.910
are less secure than absentee ballots

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.600
that require an excuse.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.390
President Trump says that
there are very strict protocols

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:37.840
for absentee ballots,

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:39.820
but that these strict security protocols

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:41.940
aren't used for mail-in ballots.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:45.590
And this is strictly
speaking, not factual.

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:48.140
The states don't have two separate systems

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.300
for voters who will be absent
from their voting district

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.450
and those who prefer to vote by mail.

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:56.410
And really each state has
its own security protocols

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:57.460
for mailed ballots.

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:00.220
And here's a rundown of most
important security features.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.920
One is that a ballot can't
be mailed to a person

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:05.670
unless they are an eligible
and registered voter.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:08.630
This means that a person must
fill out registration forms

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:10.110
and have their address confirmed.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.270
Ballots can't be forwarded
to a new address,

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.640
which means that a person must
also have a current address

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.380
to receive a ballot in the mail.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.180
And generally ballots
arriving before election day

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:21.420
are stored in a secure room.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.600
Some states use security
cameras to monitor the rooms.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.300
Some received ballots also are
stored using special locks,

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.170
such as locks that require
bipartisan teams to open

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.290
and ballots often have a barcode,

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:35.960
and this means that a voter
can only be issued one ballot.

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:38.600
In states with barcoding,
it's incredibly difficult,

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:39.800
if not effectively impossible

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:41.590
to just simply fill out a new ballot

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:43.910
for someone who was
already mailed in a ballot

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:45.750
and had their barcode registered.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.620
And often states that
mail every voter a ballot

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:52.020
use multi-layer security
envelopes in signature matching.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:53.310
And since your verification

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:55.360
is one of the major security checks

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.080
that states use to track ballots,

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:00.310
every ballot is tracked in
an election management system

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:02.210
that tries to prevent double voting.

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:04.930
And often every signature that
is returned on every ballot

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:07.530
is then checked against
the signature on file

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:09.320
with the voters registration,

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:11.730
local officials then determine
if the ballot was returned

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:12.930
by an eligible voter.

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:14.450
Additionally, the U.S. Postal Service

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:16.140
recently sent a vote by mail kit

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:19.230
to over 11,000 local election officials.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:22.140
These kits encourage them
to adopt best practices,

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:24.950
including the post offices
intelligent mail barcode,

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:27.650
which lets mailed ballots
be fully documented

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:29.700
as they move to and from voters

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.250
and can make it easier
to identify legal votes.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:34.710
But obviously no system is perfect

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:36.850
and these measures haven't
stopped the President

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:38.600
from predicting mass voter fraud.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.040
But let's talk about voter fraud

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:42.130
and how that differs
from voter suppression.

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:45.330
Voter fraud is the illegal
behavior of individual voters,

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:48.780
whether you are fraudulently
creating duplicate votes,

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:51.660
or you are fraudulently
voting on behalf of someone

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:53.610
who you're not supposed to be voting for.

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:56.120
Voter suppression is any
effort by the government

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:58.350
or other individuals to
prevent or discourage

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:01.510
eligible voters from registering
to vote or voting itself.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:03.230
Unfortunately there's a long history

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:05.310
of voter suppression
tactics in this country.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:08.270
They can be very simple
and violent measures

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:09.430
to prevent people from voting

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:12.250
like the murders of James
Chaney, Andrew Goodman,

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:14.080
and Michael Schwerner, who were killed

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:16.790
for registering black voters
in Mississippi in 1964.

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:17.790
Other suppression tactics

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:20.020
include requiring forms of identification

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:21.730
that people have difficulty obtaining

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:24.200
or limiting the hours for early voting

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:27.590
so that people have to balance
family and work obligations

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:29.830
with the need to show up on election day.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:32.980
This is one reason why often
people argue that election day

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:34.540
should be a national holiday

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:37.410
so that more people have
the opportunity to vote

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:39.410
and don't need to make a choice

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:43.250
between going to their job and
voting in a national election

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:45.230
and other voter suppression tactics

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.200
can include trying to
disenfranchise people.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:49.380
For example, making it
a rule that all felons

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:50.490
are unable to vote.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.520
That is not a constitutional requirement.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:54.170
That if you are convicted of a felony,

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:56.230
you automatically lose the right to vote.

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:59.450
Suppression tactics can
also include using software

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:01.820
that purges people from the
roster of eligible voters.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:04.190
And sometimes all of these
tactics come together.

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:07.620
For example, Florida purged
thousands of alleged felons

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:09.020
from voter rolls in 2000.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:12.010
This was before the recent
Florida constitutional amendment

00:14:12.010 --> 00:14:13.810
that allowed felons to vote,

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:17.430
which is highly controversial
and is up in the courts

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:18.490
as we speak.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:19.920
But in that particular case,

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:21.290
later investigators discovered

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:22.750
that the system purged many people

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:24.150
without felony convictions,

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:27.100
simply because they had the
same birthdate as a felon

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:29.140
or the first four letters of their name

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:30.310
seemed to match a felon's.

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.640
And in Georgia in 2018, Brian Kemp,

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.910
who was then the Georgia
secretary of state,

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:38.540
purged the voter rolls by
eliminating 850,000 voters

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:40.430
who hadn't voted in a while.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:43.060
This was called the use
it or lose it approach.

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:46.080
And Kemp was then elected
governor that year.

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:48.520
- I'm Brian Kemp, this is Jake.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:50.860
Young man interested
in one of my daughters.

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:51.920
- Yes sir.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.670
- But apart from voter suppression,

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:57.240
which is its own thing and
deserves its own video,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.750
there is voter fraud, which is ostensibly

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:03.010
what a lot of people in
the Trump administration

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:03.940
are worried about.

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:06.070
So what is voter fraud
and how common is it?

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:07.330
Well, there's several different categories

00:15:07.330 --> 00:15:08.740
of potential voter fraud.

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:11.070
And let's start with something
called double voting.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:13.430
Double voting is when
a person votes twice.

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:15.960
Obviously one person can only vote once

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:17.530
in any particular election.

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:18.850
Now to prove double voting,

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:21.290
you just need proof that
someone voted more than once.

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:23.950
And most states require voting
documentation to be saved

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:25.570
such as signed poll books.

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:27.850
Double voting is actually extremely rare.

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:28.950
The reason for this,

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:32.020
it was just that it isn't worth
it for a criminal conviction

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:35.060
to add one incremental
vote to a candidate's total

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:36.790
when any particular candidate

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:39.270
is going to have thousands
and thousands of votes.

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:40.680
Each act of voter fraud

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:42.340
in connection with a federal election,

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:45.250
risks five years in
prison and a $10,000 fine,

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:47.280
in addition to any state penalties.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:48.980
For double voting to throw an election,

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:51.010
you would need thousands
of people in a jurisdiction

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:52.170
willing to take that risk

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:55.020
or you'd need one person
to vote many, many times,

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:57.060
which increases the
chance of getting caught.

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:58.730
Often what appears to be a double vote

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:01.030
is actually just confusion
about the person's identity.

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:04.400
In 2004, for example,
two Kathleen Sullivans

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:06.570
living in New Jersey were
confused for each other,

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:08.990
that's mistake, but not voter fraud.

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:11.600
Then you have the classic
kind of voter fraud

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:12.930
involving dead voters.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:15.840
The media frequently run
stories about dead people voting

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:17.780
only to find out after
further investigation

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:19.640
that this is based on clerical errors.

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:21.830
More often than the
clerical errors though,

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:24.580
is that people will simply
cast an absentee ballot.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:26.590
And then in the interim
before the election is over,

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:27.790
they pass away.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:29.520
And while there are certainly stories

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:31.730
of people filling the ballot boxes

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:34.320
with the names of the deceased

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:35.840
at the turn of the 20th century

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:39.020
and things like the
Chicago Political Machine

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:40.650
of 100 years ago,

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:42.910
in the modern era, this
kind of voter fraud

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:44.660
is extraordinarily rare.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:46.830
Another common claim is
that people will register

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:49.760
with fraudulent addresses like
vacant lots of storage units

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:51.530
in order to get more votes.

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:53.900
This obviously runs into the same problems

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:55.910
with trying to use the same name,

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:57.700
to be able to vote more than once,

00:16:57.700 --> 00:16:59.420
but not many fake address claims

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:01.290
have been substantiated over the years.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:04.030
And some states maintain a
list of properties and lots

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:06.510
that are allegedly vacant
only to find out later

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.570
that they actually now hold houses.

00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:11.030
This actually happened in
Missouri in the year 2000,

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:12.980
officials also mistakenly
challenged people

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:15.400
like Barbara Taylor, a Washington voter,

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:17.380
who listed her address
as a storage facility.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:19.850
It turns out that Taylor
managed the storage company

00:17:19.850 --> 00:17:21.550
and lived in an apartment on site

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:23.220
and had lived there for 12 years.

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:25.620
And her address was in fact legitimate.

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:28.430
And then there's the possibility
of felony-barred voters.

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:31.330
And this one's controversial
because in many states,

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:34.380
people who are convicted of
felonies are allowed to vote.

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:36.400
Ironically, it's actually far more likely

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:39.030
that a state will purge eligible voters

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:42.600
from the voting rolls when
they're trying to purge felons

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.880
than it is for people who
are convicted of felonies

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:48.660
from accidentally voting or
even intentionally voting

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:49.960
despite felony conviction.

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:52.420
And then finally there is
the possibility of voting

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:53.950
by non-citizens.

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:55.700
President Trump and his advisors claim,

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:58.460
that non-citizen immigrants voted on mass,

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:00.520
but there's been no evidence
of illegal immigrants,

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:03.170
really voting at all, let
alone in large numbers.

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:05.410
This could be because the
penalty for illegally voting

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:08.370
is deportation while
the payoff is minuscule.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:12.410
Again, one marginal vote
in a general election

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:13.790
and of the few documented cases

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:15.800
where an ineligible immigrant voted

00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:17.390
most involve innocent mistakes.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:18.980
Sometimes people who are going through

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:20.170
the naturalization process

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:21.810
are given voter registration forms,

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:22.930
which they turn in

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:26.060
before they have actually
become naturalized citizens.

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:27.390
As worth considering how difficult

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:29.860
this kind of fraud would
be to pull off at scale,

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:32.670
in Hawaii, in 2000, there were allegations

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:35.610
that 533 non-citizens registered to vote,

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:37.120
but an investigation revealed

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.680
that 144 of those registered voters

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:43.440
actually became citizens and
then were eligible to vote.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:46.350
At least 61 asked the state
to cancel their registrations

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:48.520
when they realized that
they were ineligible,

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:50.750
two others were stopped at
the polls by poll workers

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:51.760
and no, never voted

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:53.590
and there were no reports substantiating

00:18:53.590 --> 00:18:55.090
that any ineligible immigrant

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:56.950
actually voted in that election.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:19:00.770
So it's worth asking, how
widespread is election fraud?

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:01.960
Well, the Heritage Foundation

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:04.460
and which is known as a
fairly conservative think tank

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:08.060
maintains a database of documented
instances of voter fraud.

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:09.810
They found that over the past 20 years,

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:12.160
about 250 million votes

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:14.260
have been cast by mail-in
ballot nationally.

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:15.130
And during that time,

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:19.550
there have been just over 1200
cases of fraud of all forms

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:22.400
resulting in 1100 criminal convictions.

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:23.250
Of these cases,

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:26.570
204 involve the fraudulent
use of absentee ballots,

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:29.620
143 resulted in criminal convictions,

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:34.620
that amounts to about
0.0006% of all votes casts.

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:35.623
So when you break that down,

00:19:35.623 --> 00:19:36.720
that works out to an average

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:39.740
of maybe seven or eight cases of fraud

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:43.880
involving mailed or absentee
ballots per year nationally.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:46.170
And remember, Oregon
has been mailing ballots

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:49.700
to all registered voters for 20 years.

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:53.330
So far 50 million ballots
have been cast in Oregon alone

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:55.660
with just two verified fraud cases

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:57.100
resulting in conviction.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:20:00.330
That's 0.00004%.

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:02.140
But that's not to say that
we shouldn't be concerned

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:04.430
about the security of our vote.

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:06.620
Obviously the strength of our democracy

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:10.990
relies on our votes being
authentic and secure,

00:20:10.990 --> 00:20:12.250
but we should also probably consider

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:15.450
what kinds of fraud cases
have been discovered recently,

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:17.280
as it regards is mail-in ballots.

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:20.100
The answer is that there's
been almost no fraud

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:22.450
considering the millions of
ballots that have been cast.

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:24.160
Of course you don't have
to take my word for it.

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:27.630
You can actually take the word
of the Trump Campaign itself

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:29.810
because the Trump Campaign
is actually filing suit

00:20:29.810 --> 00:20:32.540
in a number of different states
to try and prevent changes

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:34.300
to the way that elections are run,

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:36.440
to make it easier for people to vote.

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:38.180
And in particular, the Trump Campaign

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:42.450
is actually suing in Pennsylvania
to stop the legislature

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:46.770
from allowing people to
vote via drop-off mailboxes

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:50.260
in places other than
designated voting stations.

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:51.670
The Trump Campaign alleged

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:54.350
that there was widespread
voter fraud in Pennsylvania,

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:57.150
and therefore the changes
could not take place.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:20:59.080
After a lengthy discovery battle

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:02.010
where the defendants demanded
that the Trump Campaign

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:03.880
actually provided evidence of the fraud

00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:05.660
that they claim was taking place,

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:08.940
the judge ordered the Trump
Campaign to provide the evidence

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:10.280
of widespread voter fraud

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:12.200
that the Trump Campaign claimed existed.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:13.870
And when push came to shove,

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:15.850
the Trump Campaign provided basically

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.780
a half dozen news articles

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:19.640
that sort of hinted at things going on,

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:21.170
which did not actually talk

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:23.270
about widespread mail-in voter fraud.

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:25.740
So the Trump Campaign's best evidence

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.570
basically showed nothing of the kind.

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:30.490
And notice I said the Trump Campaign

00:21:30.490 --> 00:21:32.430
rather than the President himself,

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:34.540
because it's the company that runs

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:36.560
the president's reelection campaign

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:39.020
that is responsible for these lawsuits,

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:41.740
which is incredibly problematic
because the campaign

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:43.720
can use all of the funds at its disposal,

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:47.170
which includes the funds
that people have donated

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:49.070
for the President's reelection.

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:51.300
Those kinds of campaign contributions

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:53.260
are being used to further

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:55.570
what a lot of people are calling
voter suppression tactics

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:58.580
to roll back amendments to voting laws,

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:02.320
which would allow for increased
franchisement of people

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:05.280
in states like Pennsylvania and Nevada,

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:06.130
and believe it or not,

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:09.230
the biggest verified
election fraud scandal

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:12.230
in recent history was actually
committed in North Carolina

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:14.800
by Republican operative,
Leslie McCrae Dowless,

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:18.430
who in 2019 was charged
with three felony counts

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:19.570
of about destruction of justice,

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:22.130
two counts of conspiring to
commit obstruction of justice,

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:24.090
two counts of possession
of absentee ballots,

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:26.650
perjury and solicitation
to commit perjury.

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:29.140
Operatives working for
Dowless illegally collected

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:31.160
and sometimes filled in absentee ballots

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:33.730
on behalf of Republican
Mark Harris's campaign.

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:36.340
Harris won by less than a 1000 votes.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:38.870
And as a result of the fraud,

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:40.630
the state ordered a new election.

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:43.300
Now, regardless of whether
there is extensive documentation

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:45.510
of actual voter fraud or not,

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:46.610
that hasn't stopped the President

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:48.060
from saying that he wants to do something

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:49.400
to change the election.

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:50.233
So the question is,

00:22:50.233 --> 00:22:53.080
can the president change
the entire elective system

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:54.440
through executive order?

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:56.140
And the answer is that the President

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:57.980
does not have the constitutional authority

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:00.470
to make unilateral changes
to the election system.

00:23:00.470 --> 00:23:04.400
These issues are largely
enshrined in federal statutes

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:08.210
and state law, which an executive
order can't really touch,

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:11.330
but the President can
undermine voting and confidence

00:23:11.330 --> 00:23:12.850
in the outcome of the election.

00:23:12.850 --> 00:23:15.170
And that may be what
the President is doing.

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:18.200
Recently, new Postmaster
General Louis DeJoy,

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.190
a Trump donor, just instituted changes

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.070
in the way that the U.S.
Postal Service works.

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:24.500
Reducing overtime for postal workers,

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:27.190
even if that means that mail
that's ready to be delivered,

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:29.700
sits at the local post
office for additional days.

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:31.850
President Trump argues that
this is all about building

00:23:31.850 --> 00:23:33.630
more efficient postal service,

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.070
but the timing of these changes coincides

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:38.440
with the president's outbursts and tirades

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:39.610
against mail-in voting.

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:41.290
House Representative, Carolyn Maloney,

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:44.160
recently sent a letter about
the reported operating changes

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:45.767
to DeJoy complaining that, the quote,

00:23:45.767 --> 00:23:48.060
"Increases in mail delivery timing

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.390
would impair the ability
of ballots to be received

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:52.060
and counted in a timely manner."

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:52.893
To many people,

00:23:52.893 --> 00:23:55.060
the sudden changes to
the U.S. Postal Service

00:23:55.060 --> 00:23:56.950
look like a voter suppression tactic.

00:23:56.950 --> 00:23:58.540
A majority of states reject ballots

00:23:58.540 --> 00:24:00.480
if they aren't delivered by election day,

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:02.470
even if they are postmarked in advance.

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:04.920
So a slowdown in the
mail could be disastrous

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:07.540
for mail-in ballots that
are sent far ahead of time.

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:10.180
That just simply don't
arrive by election day.

00:24:10.180 --> 00:24:12.110
And the president has repeatedly stated,

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:14.480
that the election must be
decided on the same day

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:15.570
that voting takes place,

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:18.030
even though there is
no federal or state law

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:20.520
that requires the actual outcome

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:22.400
to be decided on the same day.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.450
In reality, the outcome of
the presidential election

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:26.000
is not official until the completion

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.810
of the canvass of votes and
certification of the results.

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:31.090
The canvass means
aggregating and confirming

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:33.000
every valid ballot cast and counted,

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:36.040
which includes absentee,
early voting, Election Day,

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:39.500
provisional, challenged,
uniformed and overseas citizens.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:41.280
This doesn't happen in one night.

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:42.980
Election Day is November 2nd,

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.320
states have until December 8th, 2020

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:46.860
to resolve election disputes.

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:48.550
And this means recounts and lawsuits

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:49.820
must be completed by then.

00:24:49.820 --> 00:24:51.910
And states have a minimum of 35 days

00:24:51.910 --> 00:24:54.200
to certify who won the
most votes in that state.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:57.210
The person with the most votes
wins each state's electors

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:58.670
with a few exceptions,

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:01.760
and the electoral college
meets on December 14th, 2020

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:02.810
and cast their ballots.

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:05.100
The ballots are of course
immediately transmitted

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:05.933
to various people.

00:25:05.933 --> 00:25:07.890
And December 23rd is the deadline

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:11.540
for receipt of all electors
ballots from all of the states.

00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:12.800
However, there's no penalty

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:14.680
for missing that particular deadline.

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:16.180
On January 6, 2021,

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:18.000
Congress is going to
meet to count the votes

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:21.820
and Inauguration Day is going
to be January 20th, 2021,

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:25.280
but as promised the Trump
Campaign is actually making good

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:28.130
on the president's threats to
try and stop mail-in voting.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:30.870
And the Trump Campaign has
actually sued the state of Nevada

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:32.060
over the state's new law,

00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:35.240
expanding mail-in voting for
the 2020 general election.

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:36.230
According to the lawsuit,

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:39.050
making all voters a
ballot makes voter fraud,

00:25:39.050 --> 00:25:40.060
quote, "Inevitable".

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:42.950
The lawsuit also claims that
nailing all voters ballots

00:25:42.950 --> 00:25:45.080
disenfranchises rural voters

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:47.190
violating the Equal Protection Clause.

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:49.300
The lawsuit takes issue
with a section of the law

00:25:49.300 --> 00:25:50.727
requiring County clerks to quote,

00:25:50.727 --> 00:25:52.850
"Establish procedures for the processing

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:54.140
and counting of ballots."

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:56.100
The Trump Campaign's
problem with this is that,

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:57.460
it's up to each County clerk

00:25:57.460 --> 00:25:59.860
to establish the best
procedures for counting

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:02.140
rather than a uniform system statewide.

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:03.700
And you may agree or disagree

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:06.000
with the various
different counties systems

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:07.330
that they put in place in which

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:10.100
there was a standardized
system throughout the state,

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:12.040
but the President's
Campaign doesn't have a role

00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:13.430
in making Nevada law.

00:26:13.430 --> 00:26:15.180
And remember the
constitution gives the states

00:26:15.180 --> 00:26:17.560
the right to administer
their own elections.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:19.470
And it probably shouldn't surprise you

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:22.400
that making an Equal Protection claim

00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:25.220
on behalf of rural voters
doesn't make any sense at all.

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:27.375
They're not a protected class

00:26:27.375 --> 00:26:29.380
and I don't see how an
Equal Protection claim

00:26:29.380 --> 00:26:31.410
works in this particular context.

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:34.800
No, personally, I think as
many people of all stripes,

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:37.270
whether Democrat or
Republican should vote.

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:38.310
Voting is a public good,

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:39.900
and both federal and state governments

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:42.150
